{"prompt": "I failed my driving test for the third time and I feel like an idiot.", "response": "Three attempts doesn't make you an idiot — it makes you someone who keeps showing up, which is genuinely hard. Plenty of great drivers needed several tries; the test measures nerves as much as skill. Be gentle with yourself today. When you're ready, ask your examiner's feedback sheet what specifically to practice, and take a lesson focused just on that."}
{"prompt": "My cat died last week and I can't stop feeling guilty that I didn't notice she was sick sooner.", "response": "I'm so sorry. That guilt is a sign of how much you loved her — but cats are famously good at hiding illness; even vets often catch things late. You gave her a home where she felt safe her whole life. Grief takes the time it takes, and it's okay to miss her out loud."}
{"prompt": "I just got laid off and my savings will only last a couple months. I feel so panicked.", "response": "It is completely normal to feel panicked — a job loss is genuinely scary. You can take a breath right now. First, call your local unemployment office today to understand your benefits; that extra support can buy you more runway. Then set aside 30 minutes each morning to apply to jobs and the rest of the day to rest, exercise, or learn something new. Having a structure will help you feel more in control."}
{"prompt": "I want to start meditating but I can never sit still for more than a minute without getting frustrated.", "response": "That is actually very common! Meditation is not about achieving a perfectly quiet mind — it is about gently returning your focus whenever you notice it has wandered. Try starting with just 60 seconds. Set a timer, close your eyes, and focus on your breath. When your mind wanders and it will, just notice that and bring it back. That one moment of noticing and returning IS the meditation. Gradually extend to two minutes, then three. You are already capable of this."}
{"prompt": "I keep comparing myself to my peers on social media and feeling like I am falling behind in life.", "response": "What you are seeing on social media is a highlight reel, not behind-the-scenes. Everyone struggles with things they never post about. Instead of comparing your full reality to other people curated moments, try this: each time you feel that pang of comparison, write down one thing you appreciate about your own life. It might feel small at first, but over time you will start training your attention to notice your own progress and worth, rather than someone else staged version."}
{"prompt": "I am the oldest person in my graduate program and I feel out of place.", "response": "Your life experience is actually a superpower in academia. While younger peers may have more recent textbook knowledge, you bring decades of perspective, emotional intelligence, and real-world context that enriches every discussion. The feeling of being out of place is natural, but it fades with time. Look for mentors who value diverse cohorts, and share your unique viewpoint in class — I bet others will find your contributions invaluable. You earned your spot here."}
{"prompt": "My partner and I just had our first really big fight and now everything feels fragile.", "response": "First fights are a rite of passage in any relationship — they do not mean you are failing. What matters is how you repair. When things calm down, try saying: I felt hurt when X happened. Can we talk about what each of us needed in that moment? Listen without defending. You do not need to agree on everything, just understand each other. The fact that you care enough to worry about fragility means you have a solid foundation to build on."}
